  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 Days ...\n\nThe next couple of days (Today and Thursday) will feature fairly \nbenign weather. Outside of a widely isolated shower in the high \nterrain, sunny skies and above average temperatures will persist. \nBe sure to get out and enjoy it if you can as big changes are in \nstore come Friday.\n\nThrough the day Thursday, the cutoff low off the coast of\nCalifornia will move inland and begin to merge with a digging\ntrough coming out of the Gulf of Alaska. These will transition\ninto a split-flow trough as they begin to impact our area early\nFriday morning. Both the ECMWF and GFS operational runs at 00z are\ndepicting the southern energy moving up through UT and se ID. This\nwould put the western 2/3 of the CWA under good diffluence aloft.\nModels are still differing in just how much moisture will fall \nalthough all are in good agreement that the bulk of it will fall\nnear and west of Billings. \n\nModels have been slowly trending upwards in precipitation totals\nfor locations in and near the mountains of south-central Montana\nover the past few runs. This will need to be watched over the \nnext couple of days to ensure that this trend continues as any\nlittle shift in a piece of this disjointed trough could make a \nbig difference. What we can say with some certainty, is that \nthere will be a downslope effect on the NE facing aspects of the \nmountains. This region will be impacted by a brief period of \nupslope on the backside of the system which will help to mitigate,\nbut not completely eliminate, the rain shadow effect. Although \nsnow levels wil be dropping through the event, it appears unlikely\nthat any snow accumulation will occur below 6kft. As of right now\nthere does not seem to be a need for any product issuance in \nterms of precipitation, but if the model trends continue, the \nwestern zones of the CWA will need to be watched closely.\n\nThe other big story with this system will, in true Montana\nfashion, be the winds. On Saturday, the trough digging out of BC\nwill bring with it some breezy winds aloft. The current model runs\nkeep the strongest 700mb winds (50+ kts) along the MT/SK border \nand out of our area. With the slight northward trend of the \nsouthern energy this makes sense dynamically. This does not mean \nthat there will be no winds, just nothing strong in this \nscenario. Just ahead of the trough, Friday morning, there is a \nnarrow window for a good gap wind setup in Livingston. As the \ntrough quickly approaches, surface pressure falls over the plains \nnear Billings will accompany pressure increases over the high \nterrain near YNP. As long as a decent inversion can setup \novernight before the clouds move in, the pre-dawn hours could be \nbreezy as winds gap out of the Paradise Valley.\n\nLooking ahead, some weak trailing energy behind the main trough\ncould bring some scattered light rain/snow showers through \nMonday. Temperatures will be closer to average, if not just below,\ngoing into next week. There is also a good signal that the upper \nlevel pattern will remain very active. Any sort of detail after \nMonday is a huge question though as all models are showing wildly\ndifferent things with some pretty large features. WMR",
